RISE ASCII SCENE 7
############################################################################################################ #
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                 #
#                                                                                                            #
#   File:           superellipsoid_stress.RISEscene                                                          #
#   Description:    A REGRESSION INSTRUMENT for the `superellipsoid` SDF part (arc-85 candidate C6), not a   #
#                   demo.  The primitive has NO exact closed-form distance function, so what it returns is   #
#                   a CONSERVATIVE bound; every specimen below is either a point on the shape continuum      #
#                   that must come out exactly right, or a case where a broken bound would let the sphere    #
#                   trace step THROUGH surface.  An over-estimating field does not fail loudly -- it         #
#                   speckles, pocks and eats silhouettes -- so the whole scene is arranged to make that      #
#                   visible BY EYE from one render.                                                          #
#                                                                                                            #
#   The part grammar is unchanged: `superellipsoid <op> <k> <pos> <euler> <scale> <a b c> <round>` with      #
#   a = radius, b = e1 (NORTH-SOUTH exponent), c = e2 (EAST-WEST exponent), round UNUSED, and the            #
#   ellipsoidal PROPORTIONS carried by the part's own <sx sy sz>.                                            #
#                                                                                                            #
#   LAYOUT: BACK band (z = -5), left to right --                                                             #
#                   COPPER ellipsoid / PLUM cushion / SLATE rounded box / CLAY near-box + box CONTROL /      #
#                   GOLD octahedron + clamped TWIN                                                           #
#           FRONT band (z = +5), left to right --                                                            #
#                   JADE cylinder / AMBER transposed twin / MAGENTA blended creature / TEAL carved shell /   #
#                   STEEL squashed-and-rotated ellipsoid / AMBER intersected gem                             #
#                                                                                                            #
#   SPECIMENS (what it stresses -> what CORRECT looks like):                                                 #
#                                                                                                            #
#     GOLD octahedron pair, back band far right -- THE MONEY CASE.  e1 = e2 = 2 is the exact upper end of    #
#       the supported exponent range: it is where the solid stops being convex if pushed further, and it     #
#       is where the conservative inradius bound is LOOSEST (a/2 against the octahedron's true inradius      #
#       a/sqrt(3) -- 13 % short, so the trace takes its smallest steps here).  The LEFT one authors          #
#       (2.0, 2.0); the RIGHT one authors (8.0, 5.0), which the parser CLAMPS to (2.0, 2.0) with a console   #
#       warning.  CORRECT: two INDISTINGUISHABLE octahedra -- six crisp triangular faces meeting at sharp    #
#       vertices, solid, no speckle along the edges.  A regression shows as pinholes / eaten edges (the      #
#       bound started over-estimating and the trace stepped through), or as the right specimen rendering     #
#       a DIFFERENT, spikier shape (the clamp stopped firing).                                               #
#                                                                                                            #
#     JADE cylinder vs AMBER transposed twin, front band left -- WHICH EXPONENT IS WHICH.  Both author       #
#       radius 1.1 and the exponent pair {0.1, 1.0}; JADE as (e1 = 0.1, e2 = 1.0), AMBER TRANSPOSED as       #
#       (e1 = 1.0, e2 = 0.1).  These are NOT the same object.  CORRECT: JADE is a CYLINDER about local Y --  #
#       a round barrel with two FLAT caps; AMBER is a SQUARE-SECTION barrel -- four flat sides under a       #
#       ROUND profile, so its silhouette curves top-to-bottom while its cross-section is square.  If the     #
#       two look alike, e1 and e2 have been swapped somewhere in the field or the parser.                    #
#                                                                                                            #
#     COPPER ellipsoid, back band far left -- THE IDENTITY.  e1 = e2 = 1 with scale 1.7/1.0/1.0 must be an   #
#       exact ellipsoid: at unit exponents the field reduces ALGEBRAICALLY to the sphere's own distance      #
#       function, and the per-axis scale is what makes it an ellipsoid.  CORRECT: a smooth, perfectly        #
#       regular ellipsoid, long axis along world X, shaded with no banding.                                  #
#                                                                                                            #
#     PLUM cushion (0.45, 0.45) and SLATE rounded box (0.25, 0.25), back band -- THE CONTINUUM MIDDLE.       #
#       This is the range the primitive exists FOR: torsos, seat cushions, rounded shells.  CORRECT: PLUM    #
#       is a pillow -- flat-ish faces with generously rounded edges; SLATE is visibly boxier with tighter    #
#       corner radii.  They must differ obviously from each other and from the COPPER ellipsoid.             #
#                                                                                                            #
#     CLAY near-box + box CONTROL, back band -- THE CLAMP FLOOR.  Left: a superellipsoid at the minimum      #
#       supported exponent (0.1, 0.1).  Right: the same half-extents as a plain `box` part.  CORRECT: the    #
#       two are nearly identical, the superellipsoid only very slightly rounded at the corners -- which is   #
#       the justification for clamping there (below 0.1 `box` is exact AND cheaper).  A large visible gap    #
#       between them means the small-exponent limit is wrong.                                                #
#                                                                                                            #
#     MAGENTA blended creature, front band centre -- COMPOSITION, THE REASON THIS IS AN SDF PART.  A         #
#       cushion torso `smin`-blended with a sphere head and two `roundcone` arms sweeping up and out, all    #
#       in ONE field (the arms read as raised horns from the camera's angle -- that is the pose, not a bug). #
#       This is what a standalone analytic chunk could not do.  CORRECT: real FILLETS where the arms and     #
#       head meet the torso -- continuous, swelling transitions, not creases and not intersection lines.     #
#       Any part                                                                                             #
#       whose field over-estimates breaks the blend FIRST, so a regression usually reads as a bite taken     #
#       out of the torso next to a limb.                                                                     #
#                                                                                                            #
#     TEAL carved shell, front band -- BOOLEANS.  A superellipsoid cushion with an octahedral                #
#       superellipsoid `subtract`ed out of its front face at blend radius 0.35.  Subtraction inverts the     #
#       sign of the carving field, so an over-estimate there fills the cavity instead of emptying it.        #
#       CORRECT: an open, smoothly-filleted octahedral hollow whose INNER walls are lit and shaded; you      #
#       can see into it.  A regression is a cavity that has closed up, or one whose walls show speckle.      #
#                                                                                                            #
#     AMBER intersected gem, front band far right -- THE OTHER BOOLEAN.  An octahedral superellipsoid        #
#       `intersect`ed HARD with a sphere-exponent one of radius 0.95.  `intersect` is the only op no         #
#       other specimen exercises, and it reads the field through smaxP with the OPPOSITE sign convention     #
#       from `subtract`.  The sphere is wider than the octahedron's face distance (1.15/sqrt3 = 0.664)       #
#       but narrower than its vertices (1.15), so it slices EXACTLY the six points off.  CORRECT: an         #
#       octahedron whose six points are round spherical caps, with a crisp circular seam onto each of        #
#       the eight flat faces -- a shape NEITHER part makes alone.  Points still sharp means the clip         #
#       field was ignored; a bare round ball means the union field was.                                      #
#                                                                                                            #
#     STEEL squashed-and-rotated ellipsoid, front band -- NON-UNIFORM SCALE UNDER ROTATION.                  #
#       scale 2.4/0.5/1.0 at 25 degrees about Z and 30 about Y.  Per-axis scale makes the field NOT a true   #
#       distance function; the part machinery compensates with the conservative min|scale| factor, and       #
#       this specimen is where that factor earns its keep (the ratio here is ~5:1).  CORRECT: a clean,       #
#       smoothly shaded lens with an unbroken silhouette.  If min|scale| stopped being applied the surface   #
#       would be pitted with holes along the steeply-scaled direction.                                       #
#                                                                                                            #
#   This scene prints EXACTLY ONE diagnostic, on purpose: the GOLD clamped twin's out-of-range exponents     #
#   trip the parser's non-rejecting `superellipsoid exponents ... clamped` WARNING on the console.  Any      #
#   OTHER log line is a regression.  There is no ambient light on purpose: an inverted or missing normal     #
#   must read as BLACK, not as a slightly darker grey.                                                       #
#                                                                                                            #
#   Author:         Aravind Krishnaswamy                                                                     #
#                                                                                                            #
############################################################################################################ #

standard_shader
{
	name			global
	shaderop		DefaultDirectLighting
}

pixelpel_rasterizer
{
	max_recursion		10
	samples				16
	lum_samples			3
}

file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern				rendered/superellipsoid_stress
	type				PNG
	bpp					8
	color_space			sRGB
}

film
{
	width			1500
	height			620
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location			0 13 32
	lookat				0 1.6 0
	up					0 1 0
	fov					19.0
}

##################################################
# Painters + materials, one distinct colour per specimen
##################################################

